% DASDL grammar — syntax only.
%
% The semantic blocks and the DIRECTIVES block live in dasdl.sem, so that what
% is left here is the language and nothing else. Trackway reads the two as one
% file; recombining them happens outside this repository.
%
% DASDL — Data and Structure Definition Language, Enterprise Database Server.
% Drawn from 8600 0213-424, converted to Markdown under ../manuals/sections/.
% Section 3 gives the top level; 6, 9, 10 and 11 give the elements below it.


% Named <ROOT> rather than <DATABASE>: Trackway takes the entry entity by name
% and accepts only <PROGRAM> or <ROOT>, and a DASDL source is a database
% description, not a program.

<ROOT> {ESCAPE}:

     ┌◄──────────────────────────────┐
>>█──┴── <DATABASE ELEMENT> ── ;(1) ─┘── ETXTOK ──█<<


<DATABASE ELEMENT>:

>>█──┬── <MODEL OPTION> ──────────────┌──█<<
     ├── <UPDATE OPTION> ─────────────┤
     ├── <OPTIONS SPECIFICATION> ─────┤
     ├── <PARAMETERS SPECIFICATION> ──┤
     ├── <DEFAULTS SPECIFICATION> ────┤
     ├── <CODE FILE SPECIFICATION> ───┤
     ├── <CONTROL FILE SPECIFICATION> ┤
     ├── <AUDIT TRAIL SPECIFICATION> ─┤
     └── <STRUCTURE DECLARATION> ─────┘


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% MODEL and UPDATE (Section 4)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<MODEL OPTION>:

>>█── MODEL ── IDNTOK(1) ──█<<


<UPDATE OPTION>:

>>█── UPDATE(1) ── [IDNTOK(2)] ──█<<


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Code files, control file, audit trail (Sections 7 and 8)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<CODE FILE SPECIFICATION>:

>>█── <CODE FILE NAME> ── = ── <FILE TITLE> ──█<<


<CODE FILE NAME>:

>>█──┬── ACCESSROUTINES(1) ┌──█<<
     ├── DMCONTROL(2) ─────┤
     ├── DMUTILITY(3) ─────┤
     ├── DMSUPPORT(4) ─────┤
     ├── LOBUTILITY(5) ────┤
     ├── RECOVERY(6) ──────┤
     ├── DATARECOVERY(7) ──┤
     ├── RECONSTRUCT(8) ───┤
     └── REORGANIZATION(9) ┘


% An MCP file title: an optional usercode in parentheses, the name in one or
% more nodes separated by '/', and an optional family.

<FILE TITLE>:

>>█── [<USERCODE>] ── <FILE NAME> ── [<ON FAMILY>] ──█<<


<USERCODE>:

>>█── ( ── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](1) ── ) ──█<<


<FILE NAME>:

     ┌◄─────────── / ──────────┐
>>█──┴── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](1) ──┘──█<<


<ON FAMILY>:

>>█── ON ── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](1) ──█<<


<CONTROL FILE SPECIFICATION>:

>>█── CONTROL(1) ── FILE ── <PHYSICAL OPTIONS> ──█<<


<AUDIT TRAIL SPECIFICATION>:

>>█── AUDIT(1) ── TRAIL ── <PHYSICAL OPTIONS> ──█<<


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Defaults (Section 5)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% Global defaults and per-structure default blocks are one list: the manual
% draws them as two, but they interleave without punctuation between them, so
% one loop that accepts either is what the source actually looks like.

<DEFAULTS SPECIFICATION>:

>>█── DEFAULTS(1) ── ( ── <DEFAULT ENTRY LIST> ── ) ──█<<


<DEFAULT ENTRY LIST>:

     ┌◄──────── [,] ───────┐
>>█──┴── <DEFAULT ENTRY> ──┘──█<<


% The per-structure block is tried first. Both it and a plain attribute open
% with a word, so what separates them is the parenthesis that has to follow a
% structure word — an entity that fails on its first token yields to the next
% branch without consuming anything.

<DEFAULT ENTRY>:

>>█──┬── <STRUCTURE DEFAULT> ┌──█<<
     └── <ATTRIBUTE> ────────┘


<STRUCTURE DEFAULT>:

>>█── <STRUCTURE DEFAULT KIND> ── <PHYSICAL OPTIONS> ──█<<


<STRUCTURE DEFAULT KIND>:

>>█──┬── <DATA SET WORD> ┌──█<<
     ├── SET(1) ─────────┤
     ├── SUBSET(2) ──────┤
     ├── ACCESS(3) ──────┤
     ├── ALPHA(4) ───────┤
     ├── NUMBER(5) ──────┤
     ├── REAL(6) ────────┤
     └── FIELD(7) ───────┘


% Every structure — data set, set, subset, global item, physical
% specification — opens with its own name, so the name is consumed once here
% and what follows decides which structure it was.

<STRUCTURE DECLARATION> {ESCAPE}:

>>█── IDNTOK(1) ── [STRTOK(2)] ── <STRUCTURE BODY> ──█<<


<STRUCTURE BODY>:

>>█──┬── <SET DECLARATION> ──────────────┌──█<<
     ├── <SUBSET DECLARATION> ───────────┤
     ├── <ACCESS DECLARATION> ───────────┤
     ├── <REMAP DECLARATION> ────────────┤
     ├── <LOGICAL DATABASE DECLARATION> ─┤
     ├── <VIRTUAL DECLARATION> ──────────┤
     ├── OF(1) ── IDNTOK(2) ─────────────┤
     ├── <PHYSICAL OPTIONS> ─────────────┤
     ├── <ITEM DESCRIPTION> ─────────────┤
     └── <ITEM ATTRIBUTE LIST> ──────────┘


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Data sets (Section 9)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<DATA SET DECLARATION>:

>>█── [<DATA SET KIND>] ── <DATA SET WORD> ── <RECORD DESCRIPTION> ── [<STRUCTURE TAIL>] ──█<<


% Physical options come either parenthesised or, after the record description,
% as a bare comma-separated list.

<STRUCTURE TAIL>:

>>█──┬── <PHYSICAL OPTIONS> ─────┌──█<<
     └── [,] ── <ATTRIBUTE LIST> ┘


<DATA SET KIND>:

>>█──┬── COMPACT(1) ──┌──█<<
     ├── DIRECT(2) ───┤
     ├── ORDERED(3) ──┤
     ├── RANDOM(4) ───┤
     ├── RESTART(5) ──┤
     ├── STANDARD(6) ─┤
     └── UNORDERED(7) ┘


% DATA, DATA SET, DATA-SET and DATASET all name the same thing; the hyphenated
% and closed-up spellings arrive as one word, so only DATA needs the follower.

<DATA SET WORD>:

>>█──┬── DATA(1) ── [SET] ┌──█<<
     ├── DATA-SET(2) ─────┤
     └── DATASET(3) ──────┘


<RECORD DESCRIPTION>:

>>█── ((1) ── <RECORD ENTRY LIST> ── )(2) ──█<<


% The semicolon is hung on the entry rather than drawn between entries: hung
% there, the decision to go round again is made on the token after it — another
% name, or the closing parenthesis — and the last semicolon may be left off,
% which is how the remap record descriptions of Section 12 are written.

<RECORD ENTRY LIST> {ESCAPE TO}:

     ┌◄──────────────────────────┐
>>█──┴── <RECORD ENTRY> ── [;] ──┘──█<<


% One shape serves every entry inside a parenthesised description: a record
% item, a field bit declaration, an embedded structure, and every kind of remap
% entry in Section 12. They differ only in what follows the name, which is what
% <STRUCTURE BODY> enumerates — and a name on its own, with nothing after it,
% is a field bit or an item carried into a remap unchanged.
%
% The rename is drawn the other way round from the manual, which writes
% 'new = old'. Read as a name and an optional '= name' the choice costs no
% lookahead; which of the two is the new name is settled in the model.

<RECORD ENTRY> {ESCAPE}:

>>█──┬── FILLER(1) ── SIZE ── INTTOK(2) ────────────────────────────────┌──█<<
     └── IDNTOK(3) ── [<RENAME>] ── [STRTOK(4)] ── [<STRUCTURE BODY>] ──┘


<RENAME>:

>>█── = ── IDNTOK(1) ──█<<


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Items (Section 9; global items are the same shapes, Section 10)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<ITEM DESCRIPTION>:

>>█──┬── <ALPHA ITEM> ──────────┌── [<ITEM ATTRIBUTE LIST>] ──█<<
     ├── <NUMBER ITEM> ─────────┤
     ├── <BOOLEAN ITEM> ────────┤
     ├── <REAL ITEM> ───────────┤
     ├── <FIELD ITEM> ──────────┤
     ├── <GROUP ITEM> ──────────┤
     ├── <POPULATION ITEM> ─────┤
     ├── <COUNT ITEM> ──────────┤
     └── <DATA SET DECLARATION> ┘


<ALPHA ITEM>:

>>█── ALPHA(1) ── ( ── INTTOK(2) ── ) ──█<<


% NUMBER(6), NUMBER(9,3) and NUMBER(S5,2) — the signed form leads with S, which
% is a letter, so it reaches the parser as an identifier rather than an integer.

<NUMBER ITEM>:

>>█── NUMBER(1) ── [<NUMERIC SIZE>] ──█<<


<REAL ITEM>:

>>█── REAL(1) ── [<NUMERIC SIZE>] ──█<<


<NUMERIC SIZE>:

>>█── ( ──┬── INTTOK(1) ┌── [<SCALE FACTOR>] ── ) ──█<<
          └── IDNTOK(2) ┘


<SCALE FACTOR>:

>>█── , ── INTTOK(1) ──█<<


<BOOLEAN ITEM>:

>>█── BOOLEAN(1) ──█<<


% FIELD(n) declares a bit field of n bits; FIELD ( ... ) declares Boolean items
% packed into one field. Both open with '(', so the paren is consumed first.

<FIELD ITEM>:

>>█── FIELD(1) ── ((2) ── <FIELD BODY> ── )(3) ──█<<


<FIELD BODY>:

>>█──┬── INTTOK(1) ──────────┌──█<<
     └── <RECORD ENTRY LIST> ┘


<GROUP ITEM>:

>>█── GROUP(1) ── <RECORD DESCRIPTION> ──█<<


% A population or count item counts the records of a named structure. Their
% virtual forms in Section 12 count nothing declared — the program assigns the
% value — so the OF is what the two forms differ by.

<POPULATION ITEM>:

>>█── POPULATION(1) ── ( ── INTTOK(2) ── ) ── [<OF STRUCTURE>] ──█<<


<COUNT ITEM>:

>>█── COUNT(1) ──┬── <OF STRUCTURE> ──────┌──█<<
                 └── ( ── INTTOK(2) ── ) ─┘


<OF STRUCTURE>:

>>█── OF ── IDNTOK(1) ──█<<


% Attributes are comma-separated and run to the semicolon that ends the item.

<ITEM ATTRIBUTE LIST>:

     ┌◄─────── [,] ────────┐
>>█──┴── <ITEM ATTRIBUTE> ─┘──█<<


<ITEM ATTRIBUTE>:

% The last branch catches the rest of Section 9's item attributes — COMS-ID,
% CCSVERSION, DATAENCRYPT, the GEMCOS family, the remap and stored clauses — as
% a word with an optional value. They are spelled out here only where the shape
% of the clause is more than name-and-value.

>>█──┬── REQUIRED(1) ────────────────────────────────────────┌──█<<
     ├── READONLY(2) ────────────────────────────────────────┤
     ├── NULL(3) ── [<IS OR EQUALS>] ── <ITEM VALUE> ────────┤
     ├── INITIALVALUE(4) ── [<IS OR EQUALS>] ── <ITEM VALUE> ┤
     ├── <OCCURS CLAUSE> ────────────────────────────────────┤
     └── <ATTRIBUTE> ────────────────────────────────────────┘


<IS OR EQUALS>:

>>█──┬── IS ┌──█<<
     └── = ─┘


<ITEM VALUE>:

>>█──┬── HIGH-VALUE(1) ┌──█<<
     ├── LOW-VALUE(2) ─┤
     ├── BLANKS(3) ────┤
     ├── TRUE(4) ──────┤
     ├── FALSE(5) ─────┤
     ├── INTTOK(6) ────┤
     ├── NUMTOK(7) ────┤
     └── STRTOK(8) ────┘


<OCCURS CLAUSE>:

>>█── [OCCURS, OC] ── INTTOK(1) ── [TIMES] ── [<DEPENDING ON>] ──█<<


<DEPENDING ON>:

>>█── DEPENDING ── [ON] ── IDNTOK(1) ──█<<


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Sets, subsets and Accesses (Section 11)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<SET DECLARATION>:

>>█── SET(1) ── OF ── IDNTOK(2) ── [<WHERE CONDITION>] ── [<SET TYPE>] ── [<PHYSICAL OPTIONS>] ──█<<


<SUBSET DECLARATION>:

>>█── SUBSET(1) ── OF ── IDNTOK(2) ── [<WHERE CONDITION>] ── [<SET TYPE>] ── [<PHYSICAL OPTIONS>] ──█<<


% An Access is not a physical structure: it names the ordering of the records
% in the data set itself, so it reads TO rather than OF.

<ACCESS DECLARATION>:

>>█── ACCESS(1) ── TO ── IDNTOK(2) ── [<WHERE CONDITION>] ── [<SET TYPE>] ── [<PHYSICAL OPTIONS>] ──█<<


<SET TYPE>:

>>█──┬── BIT(1) ── VECTOR ────┌──█<<
     ├── LIST(2) ─────────────┤
     ├── UNORDERED(3) ── LIST ┤
     └── <KEY CLAUSE> ────────┘


% After the keys, a set carries a comma-separated tail mixing DUPLICATES, the
% index organisation and physical attributes, written without parentheses.

<KEY CLAUSE>:

>>█── KEY ── [IS] ── <KEY SPEC> ── [<SET TAIL>] ──█<<


% The tail may open with its own comma, straight after the closing key paren.

<SET TAIL>:

>>█── [,] ── <SET OPTION LIST> ──█<<


<SET OPTION LIST>:

     ┌◄─────── [,] ──────┐
>>█──┴── <SET OPTION> ───┘──█<<


<SET OPTION>:

>>█──┬── DUPLICATES(1) ── [<DUPLICATES OPTION>] ─────────┌──█<<
     ├── NO(2) ── DUPLICATES(3) ── [<DUPLICATES OPTION>] ┤
     ├── INDEX(4) ── [RANDOM, SEQUENTIAL](5) ────────────┤
     ├── ORDERED(6) ── LIST ─────────────────────────────┤
     ├── I-R(7) ─────────────────────────────────────────┤
     ├── I-S(8) ─────────────────────────────────────────┤
     ├── O-L(9) ─────────────────────────────────────────┤
     ├── DATA(10) ── ( ── <KEY ITEM LIST> ── )(11) ──────┤
     └── <ATTRIBUTE> ────────────────────────────────────┘


<DUPLICATES OPTION>:

>>█──┬── FIRST(1) ──────┌──█<<
     ├── LAST(2) ───────┤
     └── KEYCHANGEOK(3) ┘


<KEY SPEC>:

>>█──┬── IDNTOK(1) ────────────────┌──█<<
     └── ( ── <KEY ITEM LIST> ── ) ┘


<KEY ITEM LIST> {ESCAPE TO}:

     ┌◄─────── , ──────┐
>>█──┴── <KEY ITEM> ───┘──█<<


<KEY ITEM>:

>>█── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](1) ── [<KEY ORDER>] ──█<<


<KEY ORDER>:

>>█──┬── ASCENDING(1) ─┌──█<<
     └── DESCENDING(2) ┘


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Options, parameters and physical specifications (Sections 6 and 10)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<OPTIONS SPECIFICATION>:

>>█── OPTIONS(1) ── ( ── <ATTRIBUTE LIST> ── ) ──█<<


<PARAMETERS SPECIFICATION>:

>>█── PARAMETERS(1) ── ( ── <ATTRIBUTE LIST> ── ) ──█<<


<PHYSICAL OPTIONS>:

>>█── ((1) ── <ATTRIBUTE LIST> ── )(2) ──█<<


% The attribute names inside these parentheses are open-ended — every physical
% option, run-time parameter and database option in the manual lands here — so
% they are matched as identifiers rather than spelled out one by one.

<ATTRIBUTE LIST> {ESCAPE TO}:

     ┌◄────── [,] ──────┐
>>█──┴── <ATTRIBUTE> ───┘──█<<


<ATTRIBUTE>:

>>█── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](1) ── [<ATTRIBUTE VALUE>] ──█<<


% Attribute values are read as a run of words rather than one value per
% attribute. The manual gives each physical option its own value grammar —
% BUFFERS = 4 + 1 PER RANDOM USER OR 2 PER SERIAL USER, POPULATIONWARN =
% 90 (DISPLAY), CONTROLPOINT = 2 SYNCPOINTS — and spelling all of them out
% buys checking of values this front end does not yet interpret. The run ends
% where the enclosing list does, at a comma, a closing parenthesis or a
% semicolon.

<ATTRIBUTE VALUE>:

>>█── = ── <VALUE EXPRESSION> ──█<<


<VALUE EXPRESSION>:

>>█──┬── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](1) ┌──█<<
     ├── STRTOK(2) ──────────┤
     └── <NUMERIC VALUE> ────┘


% A value that is one word is one word; only a number carries a tail, and the
% words that may follow it are a closed set of units and qualifiers. That is
% what tells "POPULATION = 1024 PACK = CLCDISCO01" — two attributes with no
% comma between them — from "AREASIZE = 75 BLOCKS", which is one.

<NUMERIC VALUE>:

>>█── [INTTOK, NUMTOK](1) ── [<NUMERIC QUALIFIER LIST>] ──█<<


<NUMERIC QUALIFIER LIST>:

     ┌◄───────────────────────┐
>>█──┴── <NUMERIC QUALIFIER> ─┘──█<<


<NUMERIC QUALIFIER>:

>>█──┬── <UNIT> ───────────────────────┌──█<<
     ├── <PER CLAUSE> ─────────────────┤
     ├── "+"(1) ── [INTTOK, NUMTOK](2) ┤
     ├── OR(3) ── [INTTOK, NUMTOK](4) ─┤
     └── ( ── [IDNTOK, RESTOK](5) ── ) ┘


<PER CLAUSE>:

>>█── PER(1) ── [<BUFFER KIND>] ── USER(2) ──█<<


<BUFFER KIND>:

>>█──┬── RANDOM(1) ┌──█<<
     └── SERIAL(2) ┘


<UNIT>:

>>█──┬── WORDS(1) ────────┌──█<<
     ├── BLOCKS(2) ───────┤
     ├── RECORDS(3) ──────┤
     ├── ENTRIES(4) ──────┤
     ├── SEGMENTS(5) ─────┤
     ├── BYTES(6) ────────┤
     ├── BITS(7) ─────────┤
     ├── SECONDS(8) ──────┤
     ├── SYNCPOINTS(9) ───┤
     └── TRANSACTIONS(10) ┘


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% WHERE conditions on automatic subsets (Section 11; the Boolean expression
% itself is Section 2)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

<WHERE CONDITION>:

>>█── WHERE(1) ── <BOOLEAN EXPRESSION> ──█<<


<BOOLEAN EXPRESSION>:

>>█── <BOOLEAN TERM> ── [<BOOLEAN TAIL>] ──█<<


<BOOLEAN TAIL>:

     ┌◄───────────────────────────────────┐
>>█──┴── [AND, OR](1) ── <BOOLEAN TERM> ──┘──█<<


<BOOLEAN TERM>:

>>█── [<NEGATION>] ── <BOOLEAN PRIMARY> ──█<<


<NEGATION>:

>>█──┬── NOT(1) ┌──█<<
     └── "^"(2) ┘


<BOOLEAN PRIMARY>:

>>█──┬── TRUE(1) ─────────────────────────────┌──█<<
     ├── FALSE(2) ────────────────────────────┤
     ├── ((3) ── <BOOLEAN EXPRESSION> ── )(4) ┤
     └── <COMPARISON> ────────────────────────┘


<COMPARISON>:

>>█── <OPERAND> ── [<COMPARISON TAIL>] ──█<<


<COMPARISON TAIL>:

>>█── <RELATIONAL OPERATOR> ── <OPERAND> ──█<<


% An operand is a value or a run of them joined by arithmetic operators. The
% WHERE conditions of Section 11 only ever compare single values, but the
% virtual items of Section 12 assign expressions — V VIRTUAL NUMBER(8) = 2*RSN
% — and they reach the same entity. Parentheses are deliberately left out: a
% '(' here would collide with the parenthesised <BOOLEAN EXPRESSION> above.

<OPERAND>:

>>█── <OPERAND PRIMARY> ── [<ARITHMETIC TAIL>] ──█<<


<OPERAND PRIMARY>:

>>█──┬── IDNTOK(1) ┌──█<<
     ├── INTTOK(2) ┤
     ├── NUMTOK(3) ┤
     └── STRTOK(4) ┘


<ARITHMETIC TAIL>:

     ┌◄──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
>>█──┴── <ARITHMETIC OPERATOR> ── <OPERAND PRIMARY> ─┘──█<<


<ARITHMETIC OPERATOR>:

>>█──┬── "+"(1) ┌──█<<
     ├── "-"(2) ┤
     ├── "*"(3) ┤
     └── "/"(4) ┘


<RELATIONAL OPERATOR>:

>>█──┬── =(1) ──┌──█<<
     ├── EQL(2) ┤
     ├── NEQ(3) ┤
     ├── LSS(4) ┤
     ├── LEQ(5) ┤
     ├── GTR(6) ┤
     ├── GEQ(7) ┤
     ├── "<"(8) ┤
     └── ">"(9) ┘


% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Remaps and logical databases (Section 12)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

% A remap and a global data remap are drawn separately in the manual — one
% redescribes a data set record, the other the database's global data — but
% their syntax differs only in which entries the record description admits, and
% nothing at the REMAPS keyword says which of the two names follows. One entity
% reads both; which was meant is a question for the model, where the name is
% resolved. REQUIRED ALL and READONLY ALL are read as the item attributes they
% spell, two words apiece, the way every other two-word attribute here is.

<REMAP DECLARATION>:

>>█── REMAPS(1) ── IDNTOK(2) ── [STRTOK(3)] ── [<ITEM ATTRIBUTE LIST>] ── <REMAP RECORD DESCRIPTION> ──█<<


<REMAP RECORD DESCRIPTION>:

>>█── <RECORD DESCRIPTION> ── [<VARIABLE-FORMAT PART>] ── [<SELECT VERIFY LIST>] ──█<<


% A virtual item is declared where a record item would be and is not in the
% record: its value is computed on retrieval, or held only for the life of the
% work area. The type that follows is an ordinary item description — OCCURS
% among its attributes — so all that is left of the manual's virtual forms is
% the assigned value.

<VIRTUAL DECLARATION>:

>>█── VIRTUAL(1) ── [STRTOK(2)] ── <ITEM DESCRIPTION> ── [<VIRTUAL VALUE>] ──█<<


<VIRTUAL VALUE>:

>>█── =(1) ── <BOOLEAN EXPRESSION> ──█<<


% A variable-format part labels each of a record's formats with an integer, so
% the colon separates the label from the description it introduces.

<VARIABLE-FORMAT PART>:

     ┌◄──────── [,] ────────────────┐
>>█──┴── <VARIABLE-FORMAT RECORD> ──┘──█<<


<VARIABLE-FORMAT RECORD>:

>>█── INTTOK(1) ── ":" ── <RECORD DESCRIPTION> ── [<SELECT VERIFY LIST>] ──█<<


% SELECT decides which records the remap returns, VERIFY which it will store.

<SELECT VERIFY LIST>:

     ┌◄──────── [,] ──────┐
>>█──┴── <SELECT VERIFY> ─┘──█<<


<SELECT VERIFY>:

>>█── [SELECT, VERIFY](1) ── <BOOLEAN EXPRESSION> ──█<<


% A logical database names the structures a program may see — data sets, or the
% remaps that redescribe them, each with the sets it may be reached by. A remap
% is reachable no other way.

<LOGICAL DATABASE DECLARATION>:

>>█── <DATABASE WORD> ── ((1) ── <STRUCTURE LIST> ── )(2) ── [<LOGICAL DATABASE OPTION>] ──█<<


<DATABASE WORD>:

>>█──┬── DATABASE(1) ─┌──█<<
     └── DATA-BASE(2) ┘


<LOGICAL DATABASE OPTION>:

>>█── GUARDFILE(1) ── [<IS OR EQUALS>] ── <FILE TITLE> ──█<<


<STRUCTURE LIST>:

     ┌◄────────── [,] ───────────┐
>>█──┴── <STRUCTURE LIST ENTRY> ─┘──█<<


<STRUCTURE LIST ENTRY>:

>>█── IDNTOK(1) ── [<RENAME>] ── [<SET PART>] ──█<<


<SET PART>:

>>█── ((1) ──┬── ALL(2) ──────────────────────┌── )(5) ──█<<
             ├── NONE(3) ─────────────────────┤
             └── [SET, SETS](4) ── <SET LIST> ┘


<SET LIST>:

     ┌◄──────── [,] ───────┐
>>█──┴── <SET LIST ENTRY> ─┘──█<<


<SET LIST ENTRY>:

>>█── IDNTOK(1) ── [<RENAME>] ──█<<
